<!--
  XSIEM custom rules
  ==================
  Additive escalation rules for the XSIEM dashboard.

  Why these exist
  +++++++++++++++
  The XSIEM backend maps Wazuh rule levels to dashboard severities:
      level >= 12  -> HIGH   (triggers email alerts + toast notifications)
      level >= 7   -> MEDIUM
      else         -> LOW

  With the stock Wazuh 4.9.2 ruleset the brute-force path only reaches level 5
  ("Windows Logon Failure" 60105 / "Logon Failure - Unknown user or bad
  password" 60122), so it would surface as LOW and the HIGH severity code path
  in XSIEM would never fire.

  These rules ADD companion HIGH severity alerts. They do not modify, disable
  or override any stock rule - the original level 5 alerts are still produced
  and still indexed.

  To revert: delete this file, remove the bind mount from
  docker-compose.override.yml, and restart the manager.

  Rule id range 100100+ follows the local rules convention already used by
  /var/ossec/etc/rules/local_rules.xml
-->

<group name="xsiem,authentication,attack,">

  <!--
    Windows logon failure burst -> HIGH.

    WHY <if_matched_sid>60122</if_matched_sid> AND NOT 60105:
    60105 is a PARENT rule. Its children 60122-60129 match the 4625 event
    first and consume it, so 60105 is never the terminal match. A Wazuh
    if_matched_sid counter only increments for the rule that actually matched,
    so a counter on 60105 stays at zero and the rule never fires. This was
    confirmed empirically: 21 failed logons produced 21x rule 60122 and 0
    alerts for the original 100100.

    60122 ("Logon Failure - Unknown user or bad password") IS the terminal
    match for a 4625 event, so counting on 60122 works.

    WHY THE STOCK RULE 60204 STOPS FIRING:
    Wazuh 4.9.2 keeps ONE composite frequency context per matched event
    stream. Every frequency rule that counts the same events competes for
    that single counter, so only one of them can fire. Stock 60204
    ("Multiple Windows Logon Failures", level 10 = MEDIUM) counts the same
    4625 events and is starved by this level 12 rule. Measured over 8+ bursts:
    with <if_matched_group>authentication_failed</if_matched_group> the winner
    was non-deterministic (100100 won 7 times, 60204 won once). With
    <if_matched_sid>60122</if_matched_sid> the counter is owned by this rule
    alone and it fired 4 out of 4 consecutive bursts.

    The <same_field> element is copied verbatim from the stock 60204 rule.
  -->
  <rule id="100100" level="12" frequency="8" timeframe="120">
    <if_matched_sid>60122</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>win.eventdata.ipAddress</same_field>
    <description>XSIEM: Windows authentication failure burst (possible brute force)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>authentication_failed,attack,brute_force,</group>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    SSH brute force.
    Parent rule 5712 = "sshd: brute force trying to get access to the system"
    (stock level 10, already frequency-correlated). Escalated to HIGH.

    NOTE: this only fires for syslog-format sshd messages. The Windows
    OpenSSH server writes to the OpenSSH/Operational event channel instead,
    so on a Windows target use rule 100100 above.
  -->
  <rule id="100101" level="12">
    <if_sid>5712</if_sid>
    <description>XSIEM: SSH brute force attempt detected</description>
    <group>attack,authentication_failed,brute_force,</group>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

</group>
